{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-152213",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{3}{8}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{8}=\\frac{(2\\times 8)+3}{8}=\\frac{19}{8}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{19}{8}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($4$) and the second fraction's denominator ($8$) have a common \nfactor of $4.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{19}{8} & =\\frac{4}{8}\\times \\frac{19}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 19 \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 19}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $19.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
